WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:06.600
Hi, I’m Mark L. Mazzeo, director and co-writer
of THE LAST WHITE MAN [now on Kickstarter].

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.950
Today I want to talk to you about some DIY
here that we made over the years and how it’s

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.030
helped us grow as filmmakers.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:15.860
So what we’ve built here you can see is
a dolly.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:23.900
It’s made out of plywood, skateboard wheels
and aluminum angle iron.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.440
A sheet of three-quarter-inch plywood cut
in half and doubled.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.340
For weight it’s big enough for operator
and camera with a tripod that is full footprint

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.980
can fit and you put it down here on these
PVC pipe and it will roll along.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.390
So PVC pipe comes in 10-foot lengths.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:44.350
The dolly is about 4-feet long.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:49.280
So that means on one 10-foot length, we get
about a 6-foot move.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.100
We decided for SAY SOMETHING we wanted to
buy a second set of pipes.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.260
So we can have 20-feet of PVC pipe which would
give us a 16-foot move.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:03.519
To connect them, we bought these smaller pieces
of PVC and just taped with some painter’s

00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:09.090
tape around the center and that fits nice
and snug into the PVC and you just connect

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.090
them.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.579
So this style we found plans for online.

00:01:11.579 --> 00:01:14.649
I’m sure you can Google DIY Dolly and find
and find something similar.

00:01:14.649 --> 00:01:17.770
The next two designs are things we came up
with on our own.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:23.329
So these are DIY light panels that we made
for our feature film SOMETHING BETTER COMES

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:24.329
ALONG.

00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:32.060
It’s basically just a little piece of scrap
two-by-four, some LED light bars that we bought

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.060
at Home Depot.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.500
I think they were less than 10 bucks each
and a little quarter 20 thread that we put

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.960
into the bottom so you can put it on a light
stand.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:47.000
Basically, you turn each light on you can
see that you’re not getting much light out

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.170
of these.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:54.390
But when you’re on location and it’s dark
and you don’t have any access to power,

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.259
these really helped us out in those situations.

00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:01.630
There are obviously some problems with them
where each individual light bar has a different

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.150
color temperature.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:08.509
To be honest, these days light panels you
can get for pretty cheap on Amazon.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.870
They work really well just as well as expensive
ones.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.730
So this is the kind of thing you wouldn’t
really need anymore.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:19.290
But if you’re really strapped, it can really
help out.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:25.370
So this is our latest creation, it’s a car
mount made mostly of scrap wood.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:31.000
The only thing we bought when we made this
was these threaded rods, nuts and bolts and

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:37.670
these little paint rollers that we use as
pads so we don’t scratch the car.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:45.310
So what you do is right here we have a two-by-six
and a piece of I believe half-inch plywood.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.580
You can put your tripod head right in there.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.860
You screw it in, tripod head here, camera
on top.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:59.330
Now I’d recommend if you do something like
this, you want to ratchet strap down, tie

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:04.370
it off, just anything you can do to make sure
your camera is not falling off.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.220
We’ve had pretty good success with this.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.220
We’ve used it on the sizzle reel for THE
LAST WHITE MAN.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.490
You can see the shots of Carl driving his
car.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:15.050
We’re all done with this and it looks really
good.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:18.830
You wouldn’t know the difference between
this and a professional hostess rig.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:23.510
Throughout the years, part of our growth as
filmmakers has evolved using our own ingenuity

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.580
to solve problems.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:31.100
So it’s not about the gear, it’s about
getting the shot you need as cheaply and efficiently

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:36.620
as possible and part of that has been building
this gear when we haven’t had money to buy

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.470
or rent anything else.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:45.810
But it’s also taught us to think outside
the box and to do the best we can with whatever

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:46.810
we have.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:49.733
And I think that will help us going forward
on THE LAST WHITE MAN [now on Kickstarter]

00:03:49.733 --> 00:03:50.650
and any other projects we have.

